# Every variable in this file is OPTIONAL. The app has no database and no
# required configuration: `npm run dev` works with no .env file at all.
#
# The only environment variables the app reads are the three below, all in
# lib/peer-session.ts. (process.env.NODE_ENV is also read there and in
# next.config.ts, but Next.js sets that itself — never set it by hand.)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional TURN relay
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WebRTC first tries to connect the two browsers directly, using public STUN
# servers to discover each side's public address. When both peers sit behind
# symmetric NAT (common on mobile carriers and strict corporate networks),
# no direct path exists and the connection fails. A TURN server fixes that by
# relaying the encrypted traffic between the peers. If you leave these unset,
# the app still works for the large majority of network pairs — it just has
# no fallback when a direct path cannot be established.
#
# SECURITY NOTE: NEXT_PUBLIC_* values are embedded into the client JavaScript
# bundle at build time. Anyone who loads the page can read them. Do not put a
# long-lived TURN credential here for a public deployment — use a TURN server
# that issues short-lived, time-limited credentials (e.g. coturn's REST API /
# ephemeral auth) or accept that the credential is public.

# TURN server URL, e.g. "turn:turn.example.com:3478" or "turns:...:5349"
#NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_URL=

# Credentials for the TURN server above (only read when the URL is set).
#NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_USERNAME=
#NEXT_PUBLIC_TURN_CREDENTIAL=
